\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a positive integer $N$, let $\omega(N)$ be the number of its distinct prime divisors. Consider $n,k\in\mathbb N$, so the consecutive block $n+1,\ldots,n+k$ lies above one.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
If the interval $[n+1,n+k]$ contains no prime, must \[\omega\bigl((n+1)\cdots(n+k)\bigr)\ge k?\] \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Must a consecutive block of composite numbers have at least as many different prime factors collectively as it has terms?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8700006 (AMR-086-0006). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Michel Waldschmidt, Open Diophantine Problems, arXiv:math/0312440v2 (24 January 2004), Conjecture 1.7. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0312440}.
\end{description}
